# Git LFS, for the file types that are inputs and are large.
#
# WHY. Twenty-one .geojson files are 106 MB, which is 62% of everything tracked
# in this repository -- all the Python in it comes to about 8 MB. They cannot
# simply be ignored: every one is read by a script, and the project has already
# paid the price of a missing GIS export once, in the dune-line files that make
# the current RoadSetback CSVs impossible to reproduce from the repo. So they
# are tracked, but stored as LFS pointers rather than as blobs in the history.
#
# The immediate pressure is data/hatteras_init/5-scr/transect_domains/
# CoastSat_transect_layer.geojson at 80 MB. GitHub warns above 50 MB and
# REFUSES above 100, so that file has roughly 20 MB of headroom before a push
# fails outright rather than warning.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT DO. It applies from the next time a matching file
# is added or modified. The 21 already in the history stay as ordinary blobs
# until someone rewrites it -- this config stops the problem growing, it does
# not shrink what is there. Converting the existing ones means git lfs migrate,
# which rewrites history on a branch already pushed to UNC-CECL/CASCADE, so it
# needs to be agreed and done in one pass with everyone re-cloning after.
#
# EVERY COLLABORATOR NEEDS GIT LFS INSTALLED (`git lfs install`, once per
# machine). Without it a matching file checks out as a short text pointer
# instead of its contents, and a script reading it fails with a parse error
# rather than a missing-file error, which is the more confusing of the two.
*.geojson filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Not listed here on purpose:
#   *.npy   the init domain arrays are gitignored; the only ones tracked are
#           the ~19 KB shoreline matrices, which are small and diff-irrelevant
#   *.png   gitignored repository-wide; only the PDF of each figure is tracked
#   *.pdf   38 files, 6.6 MB total -- small enough that LFS would cost more in
#           setup friction than it saves
#   *.csv   774 files, 43 MB, and they are text that diffs line by line, which
#           is most of why they are worth versioning at all
#
# NO `text eol=` RULES HERE EITHER, deliberately. This is a Windows checkout and
# git reports LF-to-CRLF conversion on nearly every text file it touches. A
# normalisation rule would re-write line endings across hundreds of tracked
# files on the next checkout and show them all as modified, which buries any
# real change. If line endings are ever worth normalising it should be its own
# commit, done knowingly, not a side effect of configuring LFS.
